!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/class2/schema/invalid/a-definition-mark-is-not-an-annotation-expected.tn"
!!schema:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/schema-sidecar.tn"
{
  spec: "[TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2, [TSON-SCHEMA] §6, [TSON-SCHEMA] §12.1"
  description: """
    How a record may be realised is written as a word after `=>`, never as an annotation. §6 fixes the
    home of a fact -- an annotation is the right home exactly when the mark changes no value's validity
    -- and this one changes it: erase it and a direct instance becomes readable at every position typed
    by the record.

    The governing meta declares no type of that name, so this is the ordinary unresolved-name error of
    §3.3.3 -- the footing `@sealed` and `@discriminator` are already on, and there is no name left to
    reserve once the mark is a word the grammar reads.

    What the vector guards is that the document fails at all. A processor keeping the retired
    declaration would resolve the name, park it in the author-annotation channel, and leave the record
    OPEN -- silently admitting the values the author wrote the mark to exclude.
    """
  meta: "meta.tn"
  import: ["core.tn"]
  error: {
    category: resolver
  }
}
